  "facts": {
    "value": "The resident lives in a 2-bedroom flat with her partner and 2 young children. She reported mould to the landlord on 16 October 2023. She said the landlord delayed addressing the mould which affected her and her children's health and damaged personal belongings. The resident said compensation provided by the landlord was not enough to replace mould damaged belongings, complete redecoration or address the impact on their health. On 28 May 2024, the resident raised a complaint stating she reported mould in October 2023, the landlord completed a mould spray treatment but the mould came back, she was pregnant and the mould was affecting her and her 1-year-old son's health, mould in kitchen, bedrooms, toilet and living room damaged wallpaper and personal belongings. The landlord issued its stage 1 complaint response on 10 June 2024, acknowledging it completed a specialist damp and mould survey on 7 December 2023 and mould treatment on 9 April 2024. On 30 October 2024, the resident escalated her complaint as little work had been completed and she was worried about her small child and 5-week-old baby who both had respiratory issues. The landlord provided its stage 2 response on 21 November 2024, offering £300 compensation for service failure. On 30 January 2025, the landlord offered a further £1000 compensation for delay, distress and inconvenience. On 10 February 2025, the landlord told its insurer to consider compensation for the resident's mould damaged items.",
